#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161726 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e960a4-872c-55c0-8439-60426671f5cf" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Definition of Integer Divisibility"}] :question/id #uuid "ada57336-8fdb-584d-b03c-7b7e31cbf55a" :question/math-academy-id "e-14981" :question/problem "Fill in the blanks so that the following reasoning is correct:\n\n> The notation $3 \\mid 18$ means $3/divides//18$ It is equivalent to the statement that there exists\n> $an//integer$ such that $18 = 3.k///$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a \\mid b$ means $a$ divides $b$. So, $3 \\mid 18$ means $3$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that\n$b=a k$.\n\nTherefore, $3 \\mid 18$ is equivalent to the statement that there exists $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{an integer}}$ $k$ such that\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}18 = 3k}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161727 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "05d3b242-3ecd-54d6-a763-925e2baada2c" :knowledge-point/title "Stating the Definition of Non-Divisibility"}] :question/id #uuid "1df938d2-db9c-5335-9f54-e44c40659992" :question/math-academy-id "e-14982" :question/problem "Fill in the blanks so that the following reasoning is correct:\n\n> The notation $5 \\not\\mid 16$ means $5doesnotdivides//16$ It is equivalent to the statement that for $\\[6pt]$ $////forany$\n> $an//integer$ we have $16 = / = 5.k///$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a \\not\\mid b$ means $a$ does not divide $b$. So, $5 \\not\\mid 16$ means $5$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k$, we have that\n\n$$\nb \\neq a k\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\not\\mid 16$ is equivalent to the statement that for $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{any}}$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{integer}}$ $k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16 \\neq 5k}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161728 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/id #uuid "2b06423e-99a9-5ac2-a413-93aa71ac4fbe" :question/math-academy-id "e-14983" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y$?\n\n1. $4 \\mid (6x+9y)$\n2. $5 \\mid (6x+9y)$\n3. $3 \\mid (6x+9y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a \\mid b$ means $a$ divides $b$.\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that\n$b=a k$.\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1$, we have $6(0)+9(1) = 9$, which is not divisible by $4$.\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0$, we have $6(1)+9(0) = 6$, which is not divisible by $5$.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that\n$6x+9y = 3(2x+3y)$.\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3 \\mid (6x+9y)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161925 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "559e653a-2ab9-54a8-84e3-02bf106a8f47" :knowledge-point/title "Inferring Divisibility in Algebraic Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "855e9e4d-6704-5a8e-baa9-470f1755bdeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186159981} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7316d4-9b3a-5fd7-820a-cf143ce96369" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045500 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #